# ----- Database (Neon Postgres) -----
# Copy to `.env` and replace with your Neon connection strings.
# Work against the Neon *development* branch here; production gets its own branch and its own
# values in the deployment environment.
#
# Both are on the Neon dashboard under "Connect". They differ only in the host:
#   pooled  -> ...-pooler.<region>.aws.neon.tech
#   direct  -> ...<region>.aws.neon.tech   (no "-pooler")
#
# Both carry `sslmode=verify-full`, not the `sslmode=require` Neon's dashboard hands you. The two
# behave identically today — node-postgres treats `require` as an alias for `verify-full` — but it
# emits a deprecation warning on `require`, because in pg v9 that mode adopts libpq semantics and
# stops verifying the certificate. Writing `verify-full` pins the behaviour we already have. It also
# keeps the warning out of Next.js's dev overlay: it fires once per Node process, so it can only
# ever land on the first request after a server start, which is what made it look like a page bug.

# Pooled connection, used by Prisma Client at runtime (src/lib/prisma.ts).
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST-pooler.REGION.aws.neon.tech/devstash?sslmode=verify-full"

# Direct (unpooled) connection, used by the Prisma CLI for migrations and the seed.
# Migrations take session-level locks that the PgBouncer pooler cannot serve, so this must not
# be the -pooler host.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST.REGION.aws.neon.tech/devstash?sslmode=verify-full"

# ----- Auth (NextAuth v5) -----
# Signs and encrypts the session JWT. Generate one with `npx auth secret` or
# `openssl rand -base64 32`. Every deployment needs its own — rotating it invalidates all sessions.
AUTH_SECRET=""

# GitHub OAuth app credentials: https://github.com/settings/developers
# The provider infers these names from the `AUTH_<PROVIDER>_ID` / `_SECRET` convention.
# Callback URL: http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/github  (local)
#               https://<your-domain>/api/auth/callback/github  (production — needs its own app)
AUTH_GITHUB_ID=""
AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET=""

# ----- Email (Resend) -----
# API key: https://resend.com/api-keys
RESEND_API_KEY=""

# Sender for the verification email.
#
# The code defaults to `onboarding@resend.dev`, which sends without a verified domain but ONLY to
# the Resend account owner's own address — any other recipient is refused with a 403. Good enough
# for local development, useless in production, where the recipient is by definition someone else.
#
# For real users this must be an address on a domain verified in Resend (Domains -> Add Domain,
# then publish the DNS records it hands you). That swap is the entire fix; no code changes with it.
#
# Verify delivery with `npm run email:test -- you@example.com` rather than trusting the API
# response. `POST /emails` returns 200 with a null error even for sends that later fail — the
# outcome is asynchronous and only settles in the email's `last_event`.
EMAIL_FROM="DevStash <noreply@yourdomain.com>"

# ----- Rate limiting (Upstash Redis) -----
# Throttles the auth endpoints (src/lib/rate-limit.ts). Create a database at
# https://console.upstash.com and copy the *REST* URL and token from its dashboard — the SDK speaks
# HTTP, not the Redis wire protocol, which is what makes it usable from a serverless function.
#
# Both may be left empty. The limiter treats missing credentials as "not configured" and lets every
# request through, so local development and CI need no Redis instance. That also means an empty pair
# in production silently disables the limit — set them there.
#
# Leaving them empty is not the same as the limiter failing: when it is configured and Upstash is
# then unreachable, requests are also allowed through (see `allowed` in src/lib/rate-limit.ts), on
# the grounds that an outage must not lock everyone out of signing in.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=""
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=""

# Origin used to build absolute verification links. A relative path is useless in an email client,
# and the request that sends the mail is not the request that will follow the link, so its host
# cannot be borrowed. NextAuth reads this too, so it must match the deployment's real origin.
# Vercel does not read a committed .env.production — set the deployed values in project settings.
AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# ----- File storage (Cloudflare R2) -----
# Where `file` and `image` items keep their content (src/lib/r2.ts). Create a bucket at
# https://dash.cloudflare.com -> R2, then an API token under "Manage R2 API Tokens" with
# Object Read & Write on it.
#
# The account id is in the R2 dashboard URL and on the bucket's settings page; the key pair is shown
# once, when the token is created.
#
# Leave the bucket **private**. Objects are never linked to directly — every read goes through
# `GET /api/files/[id]`, which looks the item up for the signed-in user and streams the object back
# only if it is theirs. A public bucket would make the URL itself the authorization, which for a
# store of personal context files and screenshots is not the trade we want.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=""
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=""
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=""
R2_BUCKET_NAME=""

# Unused, and deliberately so: it only means something for a public bucket. Kept as the one place
# that records the decision, and as what a future switch to public reads or presigned URLs would
# need. Nothing in `src/` reads it.
R2_PUBLIC_URL=""

# ----- Billing (Stripe) -----
# The Pro subscription (src/lib/stripe.ts, src/config/billing.ts). Create the product and its two
# prices at https://dashboard.stripe.com in **test mode** first — every value below is mode-specific,
# and none of the test ones work in production.
#
# Secret key: Developers -> API keys. Server-side only; `src/lib/stripe.ts` is `server-only` so it
# can never reach the browser.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=""

# Signing secret for POST /api/stripe/webhook, which is how the handler knows a request is really
# from Stripe — that route is excluded from the proxy (src/proxy.ts) and has no session behind it.
#
# The local secret is NOT the deployed one, and this is the routine hour-long confusion: the
# dashboard endpoint (Developers -> Webhooks) has its own `whsec_…`, and `stripe listen` prints a
# different `whsec_…` of its own each time it runs. Put the CLI's here and the dashboard's in the
# deployment's environment; using either in the other place fails signature verification with a
# message that says nothing about which secret is wrong.
#
#   stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/stripe/webhook
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# The two recurring prices on the one "DevStash Pro" product — $8/month and $72/year. One product
# with two prices, not two products: that is what lets the Billing Portal offer a monthly<->yearly
# switch. Copy each price id (`price_…`, not the product id) from the product page.
STRIPE_PRICE_ID_MONTHLY=""
STRIPE_PRICE_ID_YEARLY=""

# Unused, and deliberately so, like `R2_PUBLIC_URL` above. The publishable key is only needed by
# `@stripe/stripe-js` in the browser, and there is no client-side Stripe here: Checkout is
# Stripe-hosted, the session is created server-side, and the browser is redirected to `session.url`.
# Kept as the one place that records the decision, and as what an embedded/custom checkout would
# need. Nothing in `src/` reads it.
STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=""